1
00:00:01,802 --> 00:00:04,500
So now the plan is
either find ourselves
a new counselor,

2
00:00:04,574 --> 00:00:07,170
stick it out with the old one,
but go more often,

3
00:00:07,246 --> 00:00:10,081
or resign ourselves to the fact
that she's never gonna be happy...

4
00:00:10,152 --> 00:00:15,026
no matter what I do, so let's take
the money we're wasting and put it
towards a membership at Lakeview.

5
00:00:15,095 --> 00:00:18,226
[Chuckles] I'm guessing the last
option has yet to be formally presented.
Yeah.

6
00:00:18,302 --> 00:00:21,274
Geez. What's with you?
You usually run half speed
to stay with me.

7
00:00:21,342 --> 00:00:25,145
Didn't get a lot of sleep last night.
[Panting]
[Panting]

8
00:00:26,218 --> 00:00:29,588
How are things with Elyse?
Great.

9
00:00:29,658 --> 00:00:31,924
Great?
Yeah.

10
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:36,004
One-syllable answers.
You're either out of breath,
or you're lying to me.

11
00:00:36,072 --> 00:00:40,513
Yeah. We have our moments,
but they usually work out.

12
00:00:40,581 --> 00:00:43,815
Man, you got some water?
Yeah.

13
00:00:43,887 --> 00:00:45,914
So how do you work things out?

14
00:00:47,127 --> 00:00:49,495
I don't know.
We talk, someone apologizes.

15
00:00:49,566 --> 00:00:51,593
Man, I shouldn't have
drank last night.

16
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:55,302
- Hey, you mean you apologize.
- Yeah, I guess.

17
00:00:55,377 --> 00:00:58,611
- How often do you guys have sex?
- Enough.

18
00:00:58,684 --> 00:01:00,745
You do it this morning?

19
00:01:00,821 --> 00:01:02,848
You did.
You did it this morning.
[Chuckles]

20
00:01:02,926 --> 00:01:06,558
Maybe that's why
you can't stand up, huh? Come on.
[Panting]

21
00:01:07,635 --> 00:01:09,628
Elyse.

22
00:01:12,746 --> 00:01:14,681
Great.

23
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:16,879
Honey, I'm dehydrated.

24
00:01:16,954 --> 00:01:19,550
Do we have anything
with electrolytes?

25
00:01:21,998 --> 00:01:24,093
Sleeping Beauty, wake up.

26
00:01:24,169 --> 00:01:27,664
[Sighs]
This is an emergency.

27
00:01:27,743 --> 00:01:29,713
The fridge is empty.
[Murmurs]

28
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:32,342
Come on.
It's almost 4:00.
[Murmuring]

29
00:01:34,958 --> 00:01:37,326
Elyse. Sweetie.

30
00:01:38,866 --> 00:01:42,430
CallJacques.
Tell him I'm not feeling well.

31
00:01:43,508 --> 00:01:46,742
It's Saturday.
You haven't been to work
in three days. Remember?

32
00:01:47,817 --> 00:01:49,946
Just let me sleep.

33
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:53,118
Come on.
You haven't been out of bed
since Wednesday.

34
00:01:53,194 --> 00:01:56,223
- Leave me alone.
- Come on. What's up?

35
00:01:56,301 --> 00:01:58,771
No. Leave me alone.

36
00:01:58,839 --> 00:02:01,571
Honey.
I said leave me alone!

37
00:02:09,027 --> 00:02:12,466
Oh. I think there's
something wrong with me.

38
00:02:46,704 --> 00:02:49,607
She's been averaging 18 hours of sleep
a day since her admission.

39
00:02:49,676 --> 00:02:52,876
Clinical depression.
Incredibly contagious.

40
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:56,319
Every time I'm around
one of them, I get blue.
It's not clinical depression.

41
00:02:56,390 --> 00:02:59,521
Great.
Got it all figured out.
You don't need me.

42
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,895
Three E.R. Doctors, two neurologists
and a radiologist have all
figured out what it's not.

43
00:03:04,973 --> 00:03:07,808
We need to figure out what it is.
If any of the above-mentioned
doctors...

44
00:03:07,879 --> 00:03:10,543
were interested in my opinion,
they would have asked for it.

45
00:03:10,618 --> 00:03:13,214
None of them are willing
to subject themselves to you.

46
00:03:13,290 --> 00:03:15,283
No pain, no gain.
[Elevator Bell Dings]

47
00:03:15,361 --> 00:03:18,230
The blood work shows
no signs of inflammation,

48
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,998
and no one can figure out
what's actually the cause-
Ah.

49
00:03:21,073 --> 00:03:25,309
What?
Husband described her as being
unusually irritable recently.

50
00:03:25,382 --> 00:03:30,050
And?
I didn't realize it was possible for
a woman to be unusually irritable.

51
00:03:30,124 --> 00:03:32,117
[Elevator Bell Dings]

52
00:03:33,598 --> 00:03:36,832
Nice try, but you're a misanthrope,
not a misogynist.

53
00:03:36,905 --> 00:03:39,808
What's the first thing
you ask a doctor
who's referring a patient?

54
00:03:39,877 --> 00:03:42,473
Are you questioning my ability
to take a history?

55
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:46,349
What's the primary-
Not "what." "Why?"

56
00:03:46,424 --> 00:03:49,726
Diseases don't
have motives.
No, but doctors do.

57
00:03:49,798 --> 00:03:51,927
Why this patient?
What interests you?

58
00:03:53,305 --> 00:03:55,969
Give me the chart.
Why?

59
00:03:57,246 --> 00:04:00,115
Lfind your interest interesting.

60
00:04:02,691 --> 00:04:05,788
[House]
She's irritable and sleeping
18 hours a day.

61
00:04:05,864 --> 00:04:07,799
What's interesting about that?

62
00:04:07,868 --> 00:04:10,931
Hypersomnia's usually accompanied
by irritability in depressed patients.

63
00:04:11,008 --> 00:04:13,308
True, but not relevant.
She's not depressed.

64
00:04:13,379 --> 00:04:16,408
Hello. She's sleeping
18 hours a day.
Fever.

65
00:04:16,485 --> 00:04:18,478
Clinical depression
does not cause fever.

66
00:04:18,556 --> 00:04:20,992
- She could be sick and depressed.
- She's sick.

67
00:04:21,061 --> 00:04:22,996
Oh, damn it.
Why didn't I think of that?

68
00:04:23,065 --> 00:04:25,968
- Yeah, that's what I meant.
- Elevated sed rate indicates inflammation.

69
00:04:26,038 --> 00:04:28,600
Hypersomnia and personality changes
point toward the brain.

70
00:04:28,677 --> 00:04:30,339
Not the spleen?

71
00:04:30,414 --> 00:04:32,509
Thank goodness we hired
a neurologist.

72
00:04:32,585 --> 00:04:34,612
Brain symptoms. Hmm.
Could this be a brain problem?

73
00:04:34,689 --> 00:04:37,182
No other systemic signs of inflammation.
Probably not vasculitis.

74
00:04:37,261 --> 00:04:39,959
What about parasites?
Malaria, Chagas.

75
00:04:40,034 --> 00:04:43,165
But the patient's never been outside of
the United States- especially the tropics.

76
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:46,269
- You mean she claims
she's never been outside the U.S.
- Very good.

77
00:04:46,346 --> 00:04:49,044
Doesn't matter.
Blood and C.S.F. Smears
show no sign of parasites.

78
00:04:49,118 --> 00:04:52,716
- Has to be a tumor then.
- A tumor sitting directly
on top of the brain stem...

79
00:04:52,793 --> 00:04:55,731
that three E.R. Doctors,
two neurologists
and a radiologist missed?

80
00:04:55,799 --> 00:04:58,634
Partridge in a pear tree
missed it too.

81
00:04:58,705 --> 00:05:02,839
Redo the blood work, and get a new M.R.I.
With two-millimeter cuts
through the mesodiencephalic.

82
00:05:02,913 --> 00:05:04,848
And check for evil stepmothers.

83
00:05:04,917 --> 00:05:06,852
This much sleep
usually indicates poison apples.

84
00:05:08,091 --> 00:05:11,257
Anything else besides
the shortness ofbreath?

85
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:14,199
Not really.
It's actually just kind of a tightness.

86
00:05:14,269 --> 00:05:16,239
You smoke?
No, never.

87
00:05:16,307 --> 00:05:18,573
- Exercise?
- Eight hours a day.

88
00:05:20,015 --> 00:05:22,451
- I teach preschool.
- Sounds fun.

89
00:05:24,891 --> 00:05:27,054
Any history of heart disease
in your family?

90
00:05:27,129 --> 00:05:29,565
Not that I know of.
Take a deep breath.

91
00:05:32,172 --> 00:05:35,611
Been under
a lot of stress lately?
No more than usual.

92
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:38,549
You're probably just a little anemic.

93
00:05:38,619 --> 00:05:40,612
I'm gonna do an E.K.G.
Just to make sure.

94
00:05:40,690 --> 00:05:44,151
Do I need to take this off?
Uh, no. You can just
pull that down in front.

95
00:05:49,708 --> 00:05:52,611
Good Lord.

96
00:05:52,681 --> 00:05:56,484
- Are those real?
- Do they look real?

97
00:05:56,556 --> 00:05:58,526
They look pretty damn good.

98
00:05:58,593 --> 00:06:00,586
They were a present
for my husband's 40th.

99
00:06:00,664 --> 00:06:02,759
Lfigured he'd enjoy them
more than a sweater.

100
00:06:03,903 --> 00:06:06,066
That's so sweet.

101
00:06:06,141 --> 00:06:10,047
But I'm afraid the cause of your problem
could be staring us right in the face.

102
00:06:10,117 --> 00:06:12,349
Actually, I guess
I'm the one doing the staring.

103
00:06:12,421 --> 00:06:15,791
Of course, I can't be sure.
I'd like to consult a colleague.

104
00:06:15,861 --> 00:06:18,559
He's actually somewhat
of an expert in these matters.

105
00:06:21,339 --> 00:06:23,366
Can I get a page on Dr. Wilson?

106
00:06:25,782 --> 00:06:27,877
A tumor?
We don't know.

107
00:06:27,952 --> 00:06:30,718
- We're checking just to be safe.
- Y'all already checked for that.

108
00:06:30,792 --> 00:06:36,030
The previous M.R.I. Had a broader view.
Some tumors are almost impossible to see
unless you know exactly where to look.

109
00:06:36,102 --> 00:06:38,902
- That means it would be small, right?
- Yeah.

110
00:06:38,974 --> 00:06:41,444
Then you'd be able
to operate, take it out?

111
00:06:41,513 --> 00:06:44,451
If it's a tumor, there are
a variety of treatment options,

112
00:06:44,519 --> 00:06:47,012
but there are variables
other than just size.

113
00:06:49,329 --> 00:06:51,629
Don't worry.

114
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:53,635
Too late.
[Chuckles Nervously]

115
00:06:58,314 --> 00:07:00,580
Try to remain as still as possible.

116
00:07:00,652 --> 00:07:05,218
The less distortion there is,
the more detail we'll be able to see.
Okay.

117
00:07:14,814 --> 00:07:18,184
[Man On P.A.]
X-ray tech, report to Radiology Room 3.

118
00:07:19,457 --> 00:07:22,486
Well. That's what
breasts look like.

119
00:07:22,563 --> 00:07:25,159
Is a lie a lie
if everybody knows it's a lie?

120
00:07:25,235 --> 00:07:29,072
Well, if a tree pretends
to fall in a forest-
House, come on. They're breasts.

121
00:07:29,143 --> 00:07:32,274
They're a birthday present,
not a philosophical treatise.
Lie number one.

122
00:07:32,349 --> 00:07:35,287
She did not do that for her husband.
She did that for herself.

123
00:07:35,355 --> 00:07:37,655
She thinks if she looks different,
she'll be different.

124
00:07:37,727 --> 00:07:41,758
No. She thinks if she looks different,
she'll be more attractive,
which I have to say-

125
00:07:41,835 --> 00:07:46,208
Not to her husband.
Cosmetic surgery is so everyone else
will look at us differently.

126
00:07:46,278 --> 00:07:48,305
The same reason
you're wearing that tie.

127
00:07:48,382 --> 00:07:50,978
Well, exactly.
That was gonna be my next point.

128
00:07:51,055 --> 00:07:52,990
Last three months,
same five ties.

129
00:07:53,059 --> 00:07:55,029
Thursday should be
that paisley thing.

130
00:07:55,096 --> 00:07:57,760
It's a gift from my wife.
No, it's not.

131
00:07:57,835 --> 00:08:00,271
Julie hates green.
You bought that yourself.

132
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:03,540
You want to look pretty at work.

133
00:08:03,613 --> 00:08:05,411
[Singsong]
Wilson's got a girlfriend.

134
00:08:05,484 --> 00:08:07,009
D- Stop.

135
00:08:07,087 --> 00:08:09,250
Stop. I don't.

136
00:08:09,324 --> 00:08:12,785
It takes two department heads
to treat shortness of breath?

137
00:08:12,865 --> 00:08:16,896
What, do the complications
increase exponentially with cup size?

138
00:08:16,973 --> 00:08:20,776
I want an E.K.G. And blood tests,
including tox screen on Mrs. -

139
00:08:21,984 --> 00:08:25,787
- Exam Room One.
- You're ordering tests
to cover your lechery.

140
00:08:25,859 --> 00:08:28,591
- Interesting.
- Very tricky case.

141
00:08:30,769 --> 00:08:33,501
You love everybody.
That's your pathology.

142
00:08:35,845 --> 00:08:39,045
There were no lesions
and no mass effect that we could see.

143
00:08:40,622 --> 00:08:43,024
What does that mean?

144
00:08:43,093 --> 00:08:46,759
It means we're still not sure
what's causing
the neurological problems.

145
00:08:46,834 --> 00:08:49,270
I know some things are hard to cure,

146
00:08:49,339 --> 00:08:53,279
but what I just don't get
is why it's taking so long
to figure out what's wrong with her.

147
00:08:53,347 --> 00:08:56,319
- I know you're scared.
I would be too.
- I don't feel good.

148
00:08:57,089 --> 00:08:59,286
You feeling nauseous?

149
00:08:59,359 --> 00:09:01,829
Honey, are you all right?

150
00:09:03,936 --> 00:09:06,498
[Cameron]
She's seizing.!

151
00:09:06,575 --> 00:09:08,510
Get her on her side.

152
00:09:08,579 --> 00:09:10,708
- I need some Ativan!
- [Nurse]
I'll get it.

153
00:09:11,283 --> 00:09:14,483
- What's happened?
- She's having a seizure.

154
00:09:14,557 --> 00:09:17,357
- [Gagging]
- She's aspirating. Suction.

155
00:09:22,005 --> 00:09:24,601
[Gagging]

156
00:09:24,677 --> 00:09:27,113
[Cameron]
Come on, Elyse.
[Gagging Continues]

157
00:09:44,318 --> 00:09:46,311
[Wilson]
The M.R.I. Reveals nothing.

158
00:09:46,389 --> 00:09:48,859
- [Foreman]
That we were able to detect.
- It's not a tumor.

159
00:09:48,927 --> 00:09:52,696
A small glioma
could hide from contrast.
We could do a PETscan.

160
00:09:52,768 --> 00:09:56,468
Yes. That's how a responsible doctor
would waste his time in this situation.

161
00:09:56,542 --> 00:09:59,206
Suddenly tests and radiologists
are incapable of error?

162
00:09:59,281 --> 00:10:04,883
A glioma not presenting
on a contrast M.R.I. Would have to be
smaller than a grain of sand,

163
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:07,123
which does not
a gravely ill person make.

164
00:10:07,197 --> 00:10:11,068
- It could be just postictal disorientation.
- We would have seen
improvement by now.

165
00:10:11,138 --> 00:10:14,372
- Late-stage Lyme disease
can cause seizures.
- Does the husband care about her?

166
00:10:14,446 --> 00:10:16,780
- He hasn't left her bedside.
- How annoying.

167
00:10:16,850 --> 00:10:18,843
It also means
she doesn't have Lyme disease.

168
00:10:18,921 --> 00:10:21,050
What, love conquers all?

169
00:10:21,125 --> 00:10:23,095
Lyme disease initially presents
with a rash.

170
00:10:23,163 --> 00:10:25,656
- Mr. Clingy would have noticed.
- We've looked at everything else.

171
00:10:25,735 --> 00:10:27,567
Did you look at her breasts?

172
00:10:27,639 --> 00:10:28,902
[Scoffs]
Men.

173
00:10:28,975 --> 00:10:31,445
It could be paraneoplastic.

174
00:10:32,515 --> 00:10:35,179
She have any family history
of breast cancer?

175
00:10:35,254 --> 00:10:37,554
Her mother died of it.

176
00:10:37,626 --> 00:10:40,290
The brain, but not the brain.

177
00:10:40,364 --> 00:10:42,425
Clever, huh?

178
00:10:48,348 --> 00:10:51,752
How can breast cancer
cause problems in her brain?

179
00:10:51,821 --> 00:10:56,125
There are molecular similarities
between brain cells and tumor cells.

180
00:10:56,197 --> 00:11:00,467
Paraneoplastic syndrome
causes the body's own antibodies
to get thrown off track.

181
00:11:00,539 --> 00:11:03,408
They end up attacking the brain
instead of the tumor.

182
00:11:03,478 --> 00:11:06,176
So, if you do find a tumor,
what do you do?

183
00:11:06,250 --> 00:11:08,982
We treat the underlying malignancy.

184
00:11:09,056 --> 00:11:12,825
Once there's no tumor to attack,
there's nothing for the antibodies
to get confused about.

185
00:11:12,897 --> 00:11:15,663
- If the tumor's treatable.
- Exactly.

186
00:11:19,043 --> 00:11:21,172
[Beeping]

187
00:11:21,248 --> 00:11:24,151
Ow.
I'm sorry.
I know it's uncomfortable.

188
00:11:24,221 --> 00:11:27,489
The tighter we go,
the better the image will be.

189
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:29,553
[Whirring]

190
00:11:31,135 --> 00:11:33,970
At least it'll keep me awake.
Don't worry. It's almost over.

191
00:11:35,376 --> 00:11:37,710
I wish people would stop
telling me not to worry.

192
00:11:37,782 --> 00:11:40,651
[Click]
I'm sorry.

193
00:11:42,424 --> 00:11:46,193
My mom was the same age.

194
00:11:46,265 --> 00:11:48,200
[Keypad Beeping]

195
00:11:50,507 --> 00:11:52,408
[Beep]

196
00:11:55,217 --> 00:11:59,783
We've been trying
to get pregnant for over a year.
Guess we're lucky we didn't.

197
00:11:59,859 --> 00:12:02,125
A lot has changed
since your mom died.

198
00:12:02,198 --> 00:12:04,133
Don't worr-

199
00:12:05,704 --> 00:12:07,570
Don't give up.

200
00:12:10,547 --> 00:12:12,482
[Keypad Beeps]

201
00:12:18,063 --> 00:12:19,998
[House]
No tumor?

202
00:12:20,067 --> 00:12:23,403
The M.R.I. And the mammogram
only showed a few benign calcifications.

203
00:12:23,474 --> 00:12:25,535
It's most likely a small-cell tumor.

204
00:12:25,612 --> 00:12:27,809
It's no surprise
we're having trouble finding it.

205
00:12:27,883 --> 00:12:31,754
We should do a PET scan.
Start with her lungs,
then maybe her bones.

206
00:12:31,824 --> 00:12:34,021
Sometimes it presents
with no tumor at all.

207
00:12:34,096 --> 00:12:38,867
- How can a disease caused by a tumor
present if there's no tumor?
- It happens- 12% of cases.

208
00:12:38,939 --> 00:12:41,808
- And how do you treat it
if there's no tumor?
- You don't.

209
00:12:41,878 --> 00:12:44,405
Those 12%, no treatment.

210
00:12:44,483 --> 00:12:46,510
They were too busy
looking for the tumor-

211
00:12:46,588 --> 00:12:48,649
right till they put the patient
in the ground.

212
00:12:48,725 --> 00:12:51,720
- What choice do we have?
- Treat the symptoms.

213
00:12:51,798 --> 00:12:55,202
- I.V. Immunoglobulin.
- So we're just gonna ignore the tumor?

214
00:12:55,272 --> 00:12:58,733
Well, eventually it'll get bigger.
Then it'll be really easy to find.

215
00:12:58,812 --> 00:13:02,307
- One of you needs to check out
where she works.
- Why?

216
00:13:02,386 --> 00:13:04,913
- 'Cause the husband's not sick.
- [Chase]
Meaning?

217
00:13:04,992 --> 00:13:09,126
If it's not paraneoplastic,
and it is a reaction
to some sort of toxin,

218
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:11,670
it's obviously not
coming from their home.

219
00:13:12,941 --> 00:13:15,173
Foreman, you do it.

220
00:13:18,185 --> 00:13:22,022
Why are you riding me?
It's what I do.

221
00:13:22,093 --> 00:13:24,723
Has it got worse lately?
Yeah. Seems to me.

222
00:13:24,798 --> 00:13:26,733
Really?

223
00:13:26,802 --> 00:13:30,605
Well, that rules out the race thing.
'Cause you were just as black last week.

224
00:13:32,214 --> 00:13:34,149
How long has Elyse worked here?

225
00:13:34,218 --> 00:13:36,620
Oh, three years.
She's my best rotisseur.

226
00:13:36,689 --> 00:13:41,221
And what's that?
The rotisseur prepares
the roasted meats and gravies.

227
00:13:41,298 --> 00:13:44,236
How do you clean your grill?
[Chuckles, Speaks French]

228
00:13:44,304 --> 00:13:46,672
You say "elbow grease."
Do you use chemical cleaners?

229
00:13:46,743 --> 00:13:49,909
Ah, non. Absolument.
And chefs don't do the cleaning anyway.

230
00:13:49,983 --> 00:13:53,251
What about pesticides?
You must spray for roaches
and that sort of thing.

231
00:13:53,322 --> 00:13:56,225
No. My kitchen is clean.
No roaches.

232
00:13:56,295 --> 00:13:59,734
[Speaking French]
[French]

233
00:13:59,803 --> 00:14:02,501
I need to get back to work.

234
00:14:02,575 --> 00:14:05,945
So the fact that I'm here
asking you these questions,
it doesn't worry you?

235
00:14:06,016 --> 00:14:08,782
[Chuckles]
Look at me.
I'm here 18 hours a day.

236
00:14:08,854 --> 00:14:12,054
That guy practically lives here.
He does live here.

237
00:14:12,128 --> 00:14:15,897
I use the same detergents
for 15 years, and everyone
is healthy as a horse.

238
00:14:15,969 --> 00:14:19,135
Whatever Elyse has,
she didn't get here.

239
00:14:19,209 --> 00:14:21,202
Tell her I hope
she feels better.

240
00:14:21,279 --> 00:14:23,408
And I had to get a new rotisseur.

241
00:14:36,210 --> 00:14:39,478
- Where's Ed?
- He went down to the gift shop
to buy a shirt.

242
00:14:39,550 --> 00:14:42,350
I told him I'd stay up here
in case you woke up.

243
00:14:43,826 --> 00:14:46,764
You must have better things to do.

244
00:14:46,832 --> 00:14:49,064
I send my laundry out.

245
00:14:49,136 --> 00:14:51,698
- You're not married?
- No.

246
00:14:51,775 --> 00:14:53,573
Waiting for the perfect guy?

247
00:14:55,282 --> 00:14:58,151
Let me guess.
You've already found him.

248
00:15:02,162 --> 00:15:04,758
He threw my towels out the window.

249
00:15:04,835 --> 00:15:08,433
[Chuckles]
That's how we met.

250
00:15:08,509 --> 00:15:13,816
Freshman year, Ed came to a party
that my roommate and I threw.

251
00:15:14,889 --> 00:15:17,986
He spent most of the night
on the bathroom floor.

252
00:15:19,397 --> 00:15:24,897
He figured I wouldn't notice
the vomit on the towels
if I didn't have any towels.

253
00:15:27,615 --> 00:15:30,108
I'm assuming he came back
the next day to apologize?

254
00:15:30,186 --> 00:15:32,554
No way.
I had to track him down.

255
00:15:34,528 --> 00:15:39,436
[Sighs]
Conflict resolution has never
been one of Ed's strong points.

256
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:42,567
Nobody's perfect, right?

257
00:15:42,645 --> 00:15:45,309
I guess.

258
00:15:45,384 --> 00:15:47,250
Oh, my neck hurts.

259
00:15:48,958 --> 00:15:51,485
You've been in this bed
for a really long time.

260
00:15:54,302 --> 00:15:59,073
We're gonna do the same test
we did last night, okay?
Do you know what day it is?

261
00:15:59,145 --> 00:16:04,349
My arm itches.
It's probably a mild skin irritation.
I'll get you some hydrocortisone in a minute.

262
00:16:04,423 --> 00:16:06,723
- Do you know what day it is?
- Tuesday.

263
00:16:06,794 --> 00:16:08,991
It really itches.

264
00:16:09,065 --> 00:16:11,365
How about I get you
that cream right now?

265
00:16:19,486 --> 00:16:22,252
Oh, my God! Get them off!
Get 'em off of me!

266
00:16:22,325 --> 00:16:25,057
- Get 'em off me! Get 'em off me!
- Elyse, calm down.

267
00:16:25,131 --> 00:16:28,000
- Get them off me!
- Elyse, calm down. Calm down.

268
00:16:28,070 --> 00:16:30,563
They're biting me!
Oh! They're biting me!

269
00:16:30,642 --> 00:16:32,669
I'm gonna need some Haldol,
five milligrams.

270
00:16:32,747 --> 00:16:34,979
- Honey, what's wrong?
- Get 'em off!

271
00:16:38,024 --> 00:16:40,494
Get 'em off. Get 'em off.
There's nothing there, Elyse.

272
00:16:40,562 --> 00:16:42,532
They're biting me.
There's nothing there.

273
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:44,535
Ow!

274
00:16:46,942 --> 00:16:48,877
[Foreman]
We had to sedate her.

275
00:16:48,946 --> 00:16:52,112
You gave sedatives to a patient
who's already sleeping
18 hours a day?

276
00:16:52,186 --> 00:16:54,986
It was better than letting her
scratch all the skin off her arms.

277
00:16:55,059 --> 00:16:58,759
- Where's Wilson?
- Creepy-crawlies are consistent
with paraneoplastic syndrome.

278
00:16:58,833 --> 00:17:01,133
Onset immediately after
I.V.I.G. Isn't.

279
00:17:01,204 --> 00:17:03,436
There is a simple explanation.

280
00:17:03,509 --> 00:17:05,502
Maybe she really has
bugs under her skin.

281
00:17:05,580 --> 00:17:10,180
- Infection?
- That's what a worsening of symptoms
after immunotherapy would suggest.

282
00:17:10,256 --> 00:17:13,194
Blood cultures and the timeline
rule out most bacteria.

283
00:17:13,262 --> 00:17:16,723
Patient throws up on your shoes,
do you clean up most of it?

284
00:17:16,802 --> 00:17:20,036
The symptoms rule out the rest.
Serology rules out viruses.

285
00:17:20,110 --> 00:17:22,080
C.S.F. Smears rule out parasites.

286
00:17:22,147 --> 00:17:25,347
In the final stage
of African trypanosomiasis...

287
00:17:25,420 --> 00:17:27,822
almost all the parasites
are inside the brain.

288
00:17:27,892 --> 00:17:29,987
It's possible
they wouldn't show on smears.

289
00:17:30,063 --> 00:17:34,561
But it's not possible for a patient
who's never been to Africa
to have African sleeping sickness.

290
00:17:34,639 --> 00:17:37,736
- I'm just saying it fits the symptoms.
- She could've got it from a transfusion.

291
00:17:37,812 --> 00:17:42,412
- Or I'm just saying she could've
got it from a transfusion.
- Which she never had.

292
00:17:42,488 --> 00:17:44,081
Okay.
[Wilson]
What about toxins?

293
00:17:44,159 --> 00:17:47,689
No, the kitchen she works in
is cleaner than some hospitals.

294
00:17:47,765 --> 00:17:50,133
But they do serve rabbit.

295
00:17:50,204 --> 00:17:52,868
Rabbit fever fits her symptoms.

296
00:17:52,943 --> 00:17:55,972
Tularemia initially presents
with a rash or ulcer near the infection site.

297
00:17:56,049 --> 00:18:00,490
Not if she inhaled it.
Chopping the meat with the cleaver
could easily aerosolize the bacteria.

298
00:18:00,558 --> 00:18:04,931
- No. Then she'd have respiratory symptoms.
- Maybe she ignored it,
figured she had a cold.

299
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,097
We rejected Lyme disease because
the couple would have noticed a rash,

300
00:18:08,173 --> 00:18:10,439
but a wet, hacking cough
is just gonna slip right by?

301
00:18:10,512 --> 00:18:15,750
It's either that or she missed her exit
on the turnpike and wound up in Africa.

302
00:18:15,822 --> 00:18:18,088
Okay, two lousy ideas.

303
00:18:18,161 --> 00:18:20,688
Unfortunately, they're better
than all the other ideas.

304
00:18:20,765 --> 00:18:24,260
Tularemia. Bizarre.

305
00:18:25,342 --> 00:18:28,940
Very nice.
That's why I ride you.

306
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:40,738
[Man On TV]
Stay tuned-
Did he just turn on the TV?

307
00:18:40,806 --> 00:18:42,867
He needs to think.

308
00:18:42,944 --> 00:18:46,439
So this should tell us
whether or not she's got rabbit fever?

309
00:18:46,518 --> 00:18:50,048
For a diagnosis of tularemia
you need a fourfold increase
in serum antibody levels.

310
00:18:50,126 --> 00:18:52,926
To measure an increase you need a before.
All we have is an after.

311
00:18:52,998 --> 00:18:56,562
- A single titer over 160
would be a big clue.
- "That's why I ride you."

312
00:18:56,638 --> 00:18:59,006
What does that mean?
When I have a good idea
it's because ofhim?

313
00:18:59,077 --> 00:19:02,208
Actually, I think he said
your idea was a lousy idea.

314
00:19:02,283 --> 00:19:06,189
It has to be one of these two conditions.
I say we take our best guess
and start treatment. Or treat both.

315
00:19:06,258 --> 00:19:08,728
The treatment for tularemia
can cause aplastic anemia.

316
00:19:08,797 --> 00:19:11,427
- How come he doesn't ride you guys?
- Got a crush on you.

317
00:19:11,502 --> 00:19:15,066
- He just doesn't know how to show it.
- Get over it. He rides everybody.

318
00:19:15,142 --> 00:19:18,876
- The treatment for sleeping sickness
kills one in 10 patients.
- We start with the safer treatment.

319
00:19:18,950 --> 00:19:22,514
By "safer," you mean the one
that's slightly less likely to kill her.

320
00:19:36,352 --> 00:19:39,016
Foreman got the gang
testing for tularemia?

321
00:19:39,091 --> 00:19:42,825
- Yep.
- Probably inconclusive, but worth doing.

322
00:19:42,899 --> 00:19:45,699
So, what's her name?
When do I get to meet her?

323
00:19:45,772 --> 00:19:48,470
- There's nobody.
Give it up.
- Your lips say no.

324
00:19:48,544 --> 00:19:51,106
Your shoes say yes.
Well, they're French.

325
00:19:51,183 --> 00:19:54,917
- You can't trust a word they say.
- Solid, yet stylish.

326
00:19:54,991 --> 00:19:57,257
A professional woman
would be impressed.

327
00:19:57,328 --> 00:20:00,357
I'm thinking accountant.
Actuary maybe.

328
00:20:00,435 --> 00:20:02,632
It's somebody in the hospital.

329
00:20:02,706 --> 00:20:04,641
Patient?

330
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:07,739
No. Chemo's not sexy.
Daughter of a patient.

331
00:20:07,817 --> 00:20:09,844
She would certainly have
the neediness you need.

332
00:20:09,921 --> 00:20:12,016
I'm not gonna date
a patient's daughter.

333
00:20:12,091 --> 00:20:16,191
Very ethical.
Of course, most married men
would say they don't date at all.

334
00:20:16,267 --> 00:20:18,567
There was no date.

335
00:20:18,638 --> 00:20:21,234
I had lunch with one of the nurses.

336
00:20:21,311 --> 00:20:24,943
It's her first time in an oncology unit.
She's having a tough time- emotionally.

337
00:20:25,018 --> 00:20:27,386
- Perfect.
- I wanted to be nice.

338
00:20:27,456 --> 00:20:29,892
That's all.
I mean it.

339
00:20:29,962 --> 00:20:32,296
You always do.
It's part of your charm.

340
00:20:32,366 --> 00:20:34,232
Hi, boys.

341
00:20:34,303 --> 00:20:36,773
Mrs. Campbell's test results.

342
00:20:36,842 --> 00:20:39,711
You remember her.
The preschool teacher
with the heart of silicone.

343
00:20:39,781 --> 00:20:41,409
No. Doesn't ring a bell.

344
00:20:41,485 --> 00:20:45,926
They came in yesterday. I figured you guys
would have been all over them.
I know how concerned you were.

345
00:20:45,994 --> 00:20:49,592
She's all upset 'cause we paid
more attention to the other girl.

346
00:20:49,668 --> 00:20:52,161
You check out her ass.
I've got the chest.

347
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:55,235
The tests were normal.
Of course, that's just my opinion.

348
00:20:55,313 --> 00:20:58,479
You might wanna call a couple of guys
from maintenance in for a consult.

349
00:20:58,553 --> 00:21:01,422
You check her E.K.G. Results
before she left the other day?

350
00:21:01,492 --> 00:21:03,689
- You ordered it.
- You're the responsible one.

351
00:21:03,764 --> 00:21:06,964
- What's wrong?
They look normal to me.
- Where is she?

352
00:21:07,037 --> 00:21:10,009
- Waiting downstairs. Why?
- I was right.

353
00:21:17,558 --> 00:21:20,393
Do I have to get rid of the implants?

354
00:21:20,464 --> 00:21:22,730
Surprisingly, no.

355
00:21:22,802 --> 00:21:26,002
But your E.K.G. Shows
a slightly decreased heart rate.

356
00:21:26,076 --> 00:21:29,640
Is that a problem?
You told me you hadn't changed
your diet or exercise. Were you lying?

357
00:21:29,716 --> 00:21:33,656
Lying?
Does your husband have
high blood pressure?

358
00:21:33,724 --> 00:21:35,659
My husband?
Yeah, you see,

359
00:21:35,728 --> 00:21:38,996
if you're gonna repeat
everything I say, this conversation's
gonna take twice as long.

360
00:21:39,068 --> 00:21:41,937
Yes. He was diagnosed
six months ago.

361
00:21:42,008 --> 00:21:45,071
He do a lot of cooking?
Not really, other than oatmeal
in the morning.

362
00:21:45,147 --> 00:21:49,053
Did you happen to notice
a slightly odd taste
to the oatmeal lately?

363
00:21:49,122 --> 00:21:51,592
Wait. Are you saying that-
That it looks like your husband...

364
00:21:51,661 --> 00:21:54,724
stirred in some of
his blood pressure medication
along with the brown sugar.

365
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:59,708
You think my husband's
trying to poison me?
No. Nothing like that.

366
00:21:59,777 --> 00:22:02,943
He just doesn't
want to have sex with you.

367
00:22:03,017 --> 00:22:07,117
Decreased sex drive is one of
the most common side effects
of the beta-blockers he's been taking.

368
00:22:07,192 --> 00:22:10,790
I'm guessing he figured
if you're both frigid, no harm, no foul.

369
00:22:10,866 --> 00:22:13,997
Should've gotten him the sweater.
That's ridiculous.

370
00:22:14,073 --> 00:22:19,880
Fine. But if you're still concerned
about the shortness of breath,
I'd start making your own breakfast.

371
00:22:19,951 --> 00:22:22,114
Wait.

372
00:22:22,189 --> 00:22:24,386
What should I do?

373
00:22:25,963 --> 00:22:29,994
If you care about your husband at all,
I'd do the responsible thing.

374
00:22:30,072 --> 00:22:33,010
Buy yourself some condoms,
go to a bar, find-

375
00:22:38,689 --> 00:22:40,624
Huh.

376
00:22:47,875 --> 00:22:50,175
[Beep]

377
00:22:51,248 --> 00:22:53,718
Lab tests inconclusive?

378
00:22:53,787 --> 00:22:56,553
Not surprisingly.
No. But too bad.

379
00:22:56,626 --> 00:22:59,723
Luckily, I have the answer.

380
00:22:59,799 --> 00:23:02,065
- To what?
- Thanks for asking.

381
00:23:02,137 --> 00:23:06,908
To life itself: Sex. Anything that can be
transmitted via the blood
can be transmitted through sex.

382
00:23:06,980 --> 00:23:09,712
- Sleeping sickness from sex?
- It's not without precedent.

383
00:23:09,786 --> 00:23:14,250
I'm pretty sure it is, unless you're
talking about going to Africa
and having sex with a tsetse fly.

384
00:23:14,328 --> 00:23:18,860
A Portuguese man
was diagnosed three years ago
with C.N.S. -affected sleeping sickness.

385
00:23:18,938 --> 00:23:23,106
His only connection with Africa
was through a girlfriend
who'd served with the military in Angola.

386
00:23:23,179 --> 00:23:25,149
Boy. Where'd you find that?

387
00:23:25,217 --> 00:23:28,747
TheJournal of the Instituto
de Higiene e Medicina Tropical.

388
00:23:28,824 --> 00:23:30,794
- You don't read Portuguese?
- You do?

389
00:23:30,862 --> 00:23:34,733
Pretty sure that's what it said.
Either that or it was
an ad for sunglasses.

390
00:23:34,803 --> 00:23:36,830
Her husband has never
been to Africa either.

391
00:23:36,908 --> 00:23:40,244
Oh, stymied again.
Your logic is bulletproof.

392
00:23:40,314 --> 00:23:43,582
I think ignoring respiratory symptoms
is more likely than cheating.

393
00:23:43,654 --> 00:23:47,560
Because?
They're completely
devoted to each other.

394
00:23:47,630 --> 00:23:50,260
Because?
They love each other.

395
00:23:50,334 --> 00:23:52,269
Or?

396
00:23:52,338 --> 00:23:54,831
They're overcompensating for guilt.

397
00:23:56,213 --> 00:24:01,679
- Find out which it is.
- You want me to ask a man whose wife
is about to die if he cheated on her?

398
00:24:01,758 --> 00:24:04,958
No, I want you to be polite
and let her die.

399
00:24:05,032 --> 00:24:09,200
Actually, I don't want you
to ask her anything. Foreman,
take the husband. Chase, take the wife.

400
00:24:19,193 --> 00:24:21,925
You don't trust me to do my job?

401
00:24:21,999 --> 00:24:24,971
We all formulate questions based on
the answers we want to hear.

402
00:24:25,038 --> 00:24:27,668
And how exactly do you reformulate
"Have you screwed around?"

403
00:24:27,744 --> 00:24:30,078
Did you know she's been
trying to get pregnant?
Yes.

404
00:24:30,148 --> 00:24:34,054
After you got so freaked
about the sick babies a while ago,
lfigured that was your thing.

405
00:24:34,124 --> 00:24:38,121
But you've never been prescribed
folic acid, and you can't lose a baby
if you've never been pregnant.

406
00:24:38,199 --> 00:24:42,070
You pulled my medical records?
You coughed the other day.
I was concerned.

407
00:24:42,140 --> 00:24:47,947
You were curious, like an eight-year-old boy
with a puzzle that's just a little too
grown-up for him to figure out.

408
00:24:49,087 --> 00:24:51,114
Tomayto, tomahto.

409
00:24:52,193 --> 00:24:56,634
So it's either, uh, sleeping sickness
or this rabbit thing?

410
00:24:56,703 --> 00:24:58,696
They're both fatal without treatment.

411
00:24:58,774 --> 00:25:02,304
And unfortunately, the treatment
for both is extremely dangerous.

412
00:25:02,381 --> 00:25:05,319
Are there tests you can do?
Not at this stage.

413
00:25:05,387 --> 00:25:08,119
But each condition has a unique history.

414
00:25:09,128 --> 00:25:12,191
We're hoping your answers
to a few questions will help us.

415
00:25:12,268 --> 00:25:15,536
Sure. Whatever you need to know.

416
00:25:15,607 --> 00:25:19,171
Before the sleeping problems,
did you have any trouble breathing,

417
00:25:19,248 --> 00:25:22,277
a cough that wouldn't go away-
anything like that?

418
00:25:23,624 --> 00:25:25,617
No.

419
00:25:25,695 --> 00:25:28,029
Are you certain?
[Ed]
Absolutely.

420
00:25:28,100 --> 00:25:30,696
I've never been away from her
for more than a night.

421
00:25:30,772 --> 00:25:33,174
If she had breathing troubles
I would've noticed.

422
00:25:35,415 --> 00:25:38,979
The other condition is
significantly more likely if-

423
00:25:40,758 --> 00:25:42,853
if you've had an affair.

424
00:25:42,930 --> 00:25:45,594
Have you ever had an affair?

425
00:25:45,668 --> 00:25:47,695
Of course not.

426
00:25:47,773 --> 00:25:51,679
- Are you sure?
- I think I'd remember
cheating on my wife.

427
00:25:51,748 --> 00:25:53,775
You might be
reluctant to admit it.
No.

428
00:25:53,852 --> 00:25:56,015
I just want to be perfectly clear.

429
00:25:56,090 --> 00:25:59,688
If your wife has sleeping sickness,
and we don't treat her,

430
00:26:00,699 --> 00:26:02,634
she'll die.

431
00:26:02,703 --> 00:26:06,039
I would never do that to Ed.

432
00:26:06,110 --> 00:26:09,480
I love him.
[Ed]
Absolutely not.

433
00:26:09,551 --> 00:26:11,544
I love her.

434
00:26:15,496 --> 00:26:17,489
All right.

435
00:26:24,848 --> 00:26:30,257
All right then.
They say no cheating,
we cross off sleeping sickness.

436
00:26:30,326 --> 00:26:32,455
Any new ideas?

437
00:26:33,733 --> 00:26:35,828
Okay. We go with Foreman's tularemia.

438
00:26:35,904 --> 00:26:40,038
Start her on I.V. Chloramphenicol,
25 milligrams per kilogram,
four times a day.

439
00:26:40,113 --> 00:26:42,106
Good night.

440
00:26:45,690 --> 00:26:48,286
[Machine Beeping]

441
00:26:54,608 --> 00:26:58,240
Where's Ed?
Right next to you.

442
00:26:58,316 --> 00:27:00,479
Well, you're two down,
two to go.

443
00:27:02,592 --> 00:27:04,527
Two days?

444
00:27:04,596 --> 00:27:08,365
No. Doses. You have about
20 more days of this fun.

445
00:27:08,437 --> 00:27:11,101
What time is it?

446
00:27:11,176 --> 00:27:15,013
About 4:00 a.m.
I pulled the short straw.

447
00:27:18,257 --> 00:27:20,250
Flow rate looks good.

448
00:27:25,137 --> 00:27:27,130
No rash or flushing.

449
00:27:28,143 --> 00:27:30,773
What time is it?

450
00:27:32,118 --> 00:27:34,680
4:00 a.m.

451
00:27:34,757 --> 00:27:37,159
Do you not remember just asking?

452
00:27:39,433 --> 00:27:41,426
I don't-

453
00:27:41,504 --> 00:27:43,770
Elyse?

454
00:27:45,645 --> 00:27:48,583
Elyse? Elyse?

455
00:27:48,651 --> 00:27:51,486
- Elyse.
- What are you doing?

456
00:27:51,557 --> 00:27:53,584
- I'm trying to wake her.
- She fall asleep again?

457
00:27:53,661 --> 00:27:55,927
In the middle of a sentence.
Elyse. Elyse.

458
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:59,564
- [Man]
What's happening?
- Patient's not responding.

459
00:27:59,641 --> 00:28:01,907
Pulse is fine.
Airway's open.

460
00:28:01,979 --> 00:28:03,972
Check her blood pressure.

461
00:28:05,586 --> 00:28:07,852
Pupils are reactive.

462
00:28:07,924 --> 00:28:10,520
Elyse, wake up.
You gotta wake up.

463
00:28:10,596 --> 00:28:14,399
- What does that mean?
What are you doing?
- She's unresponsive to pain.

464
00:28:14,471 --> 00:28:17,374
Come on, Elyse.

465
00:28:17,444 --> 00:28:20,678
- Is she dying?
- I don't know.

466
00:28:31,138 --> 00:28:33,004
She's in a coma.

467
00:28:39,455 --> 00:28:43,019
There's only one way
a tularemia patient
goes into a coma...

468
00:28:43,095 --> 00:28:45,030
while on I.V. Chloramphenicol.

469
00:28:45,099 --> 00:28:48,435
The patient doesn't have tularemia.
And then there was one.

470
00:28:48,507 --> 00:28:50,534
Patient comes in
'cause she's sleeping too much.

471
00:28:50,611 --> 00:28:54,779
It takes 10 doctors and a coma
to diagnose sleeping sickness.
And then there was none.

472
00:28:54,853 --> 00:28:57,916
We still have the problem
of explaining how
a white chick from Jersey...

473
00:28:57,993 --> 00:29:00,520
who's never traveled south of D.C.
Has African sleeping sickness.

474
00:29:00,597 --> 00:29:02,829
Still have the obvious explanation.

475
00:29:03,838 --> 00:29:08,940
I made it clear.
If this guy's lying about sleeping around
he knows he's murdering his wife.

476
00:29:09,015 --> 00:29:11,178
Does seem unlikely.

477
00:29:11,252 --> 00:29:13,245
Go away.

478
00:29:16,864 --> 00:29:18,857
[Beeping]

479
00:29:30,558 --> 00:29:32,926
What are you doing?

480
00:29:34,132 --> 00:29:36,534
Checking for lymphadenopathy.

481
00:29:36,605 --> 00:29:40,237
And waiting for you.
Who are you?

482
00:29:40,311 --> 00:29:42,406
I'm Dr. House.

483
00:29:42,483 --> 00:29:45,455
Your wife has
human African trypanosomiasis.

484
00:29:46,958 --> 00:29:50,294
Sleeping sickness.
You mean it's not tularemia,

485
00:29:50,366 --> 00:29:52,928
a virus, tumor or- or cancer?

486
00:29:53,005 --> 00:29:55,874
No.

487
00:29:57,580 --> 00:29:59,709
I've never had an affair.

488
00:29:59,785 --> 00:30:01,880
I believe you.

489
00:30:01,956 --> 00:30:04,552
And I trust Elyse.

490
00:30:06,331 --> 00:30:10,100
The treatment for this disease
is a drug that's...

491
00:30:10,172 --> 00:30:13,167
fatal on its own 10% of the time.

492
00:30:13,245 --> 00:30:17,310
Which is why I need
your written consent
before I can legally prescribe it.

493
00:30:17,387 --> 00:30:20,951
Why would she lie
if she knew it could kill her?
I don't ask why patients lie.

494
00:30:21,027 --> 00:30:22,962
I just assume they all do.

495
00:30:23,032 --> 00:30:25,628
But why?
To protect you,

496
00:30:25,704 --> 00:30:28,767
because she didn't think it mattered,
it just seemed easier, because...

497
00:30:30,313 --> 00:30:32,306
that's what people do.

498
00:30:35,156 --> 00:30:38,720
If you're absolutely certain
that your wife has never
had sex with anyone but you...

499
00:30:38,797 --> 00:30:41,324
since you were married,
then I'm wrong.

500
00:30:43,106 --> 00:30:47,604
But if you think there's
a possibility that just one time...

501
00:30:48,917 --> 00:30:51,581
she wasn't perfect-

502
00:30:51,656 --> 00:30:53,819
one weekend you're out of town,

503
00:30:54,829 --> 00:30:57,026
one fight where she ran to a friend,

504
00:30:57,101 --> 00:30:59,162
one stupid Christmas party-

505
00:31:01,878 --> 00:31:04,975
then you need to allow me
to start treatment.

506
00:31:06,520 --> 00:31:08,649
Because if we don't,

507
00:31:08,725 --> 00:31:10,695
she's gonna be dead
by tomorrow morning.

508
00:31:20,114 --> 00:31:22,277
You trust your wife that much?

509
00:31:24,557 --> 00:31:26,550
I don't know.

510
00:31:30,168 --> 00:31:33,265
I'm gonna start the treatment.

511
00:31:48,271 --> 00:31:52,234
Glass syringes?
And special I.V. Tubing.

512
00:31:52,313 --> 00:31:55,342
Why do we need this stuff?
Because melarsoprol melts plastic.

513
00:31:55,419 --> 00:31:57,946
This stuff's basically arsenic
mixed with antifreeze.

514
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:01,121
What doesn't kill us
makes us stronger, huh?

515
00:32:01,198 --> 00:32:04,170
Nietzsche wouldn't have been so glib
if he'd been prescribed melarsoprol.

516
00:32:06,308 --> 00:32:09,006
"Can cause vomiting, abdominal pain,

517
00:32:09,080 --> 00:32:11,846
blood toxicity, neural damage,
cardiac arrhythmia."

518
00:32:11,920 --> 00:32:14,516
They forgot to say
it's gonna hurt like all hell.

519
00:32:14,592 --> 00:32:17,154
[Foreman]
She's lucky she's in a coma.

520
00:32:34,131 --> 00:32:37,570
How long before we know
if it's working?
It's tough to say.

521
00:32:37,638 --> 00:32:40,438
It's a good sign
that she hasn't gotten any worse.

522
00:32:43,350 --> 00:32:45,479
Does she even know I'm here?

523
00:32:45,555 --> 00:32:48,219
She knows you're
always there for her.

524
00:32:51,099 --> 00:32:53,262
Yeah.

525
00:32:53,337 --> 00:32:55,569
If she gets better,

526
00:32:55,641 --> 00:32:57,668
it means she wasn't always there for me.

527
00:33:01,320 --> 00:33:03,847
It means she made a mistake.

528
00:33:05,361 --> 00:33:07,354
I can't help it.

529
00:33:07,432 --> 00:33:11,964
Part of me- a big part of me-
can't handle that.

530
00:33:13,779 --> 00:33:16,306
It doesn't want her to get better.

531
00:33:26,136 --> 00:33:28,129
Does that make me
a terrible person?

532
00:33:36,859 --> 00:33:38,852
Yes.

533
00:33:57,767 --> 00:34:00,329
[Elevator Bell Dings]

534
00:34:00,406 --> 00:34:03,104
So you're treating her
for African sleeping sickness...

535
00:34:03,179 --> 00:34:06,640
because you don't think it's possible
for someone to be faithful
in a relationship?

536
00:34:06,719 --> 00:34:08,689
And you do?
Yes.

537
00:34:08,756 --> 00:34:12,958
And you need to tell me that.
[Chuckles]
Look. I am not having an affair.

538
00:34:13,032 --> 00:34:16,493
I had lunch with someone
I work with- at work. Once.

539
00:34:16,572 --> 00:34:20,170
I believe you.
What I don't believe
is it'll be just once.

540
00:34:20,246 --> 00:34:22,739
I love my wife.
You certainly love saying it.

541
00:34:22,818 --> 00:34:26,484
[Chuckles]
I'm sorry.
I know you love your wife.

542
00:34:26,560 --> 00:34:28,621
You loved all your wives.
Probably still do.

543
00:34:28,697 --> 00:34:32,067
In fact, you probably love
all the women you loved
who weren't your wife.

544
00:34:32,138 --> 00:34:35,599
You can be a real jerk sometimes,
you know that?
Yeah. And you're the good guy.

545
00:34:35,678 --> 00:34:39,276
At least I try.
As long as you're trying to be good,
you can do whatever you want.

546
00:34:39,352 --> 00:34:43,690
And as long as you're not trying,
you can say whatever you want.
So, between us, we can do anything.

547
00:34:43,761 --> 00:34:46,288
We can rule the world.
[Sighs]

548
00:34:58,625 --> 00:35:00,788
Mixing up some margaritas?

549
00:35:00,862 --> 00:35:03,025
Mine's a double, senorita.

550
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:05,366
That's Portuguese, you know.
Spanish.

551
00:35:06,440 --> 00:35:08,375
Uh-oh.

552
00:35:09,647 --> 00:35:11,548
What's goin' on?

553
00:35:11,617 --> 00:35:14,383
I'm recalibrating the centrifuge.

554
00:35:14,456 --> 00:35:16,449
Turn around.

555
00:35:25,780 --> 00:35:27,841
It's a very sad thing.

556
00:35:27,917 --> 00:35:29,887
An uncalibrated centrifuge.

557
00:35:29,954 --> 00:35:33,791
Makes me cry too.
I'm not crying.

558
00:35:34,964 --> 00:35:36,957
Okay.

559
00:35:50,329 --> 00:35:52,891
I told the husband he was a jerk.

560
00:35:54,772 --> 00:35:56,765
Why?

561
00:36:02,921 --> 00:36:05,187
When I was in college, l-

562
00:36:08,066 --> 00:36:12,632
I fell in love,
and I got married.

563
00:36:12,708 --> 00:36:14,871
And-

564
00:36:14,946 --> 00:36:17,041
At that age, the chances
of marriage lasting-

565
00:36:17,117 --> 00:36:19,519
It lasted six months.

566
00:36:21,660 --> 00:36:24,826
Thyroid cancer
metastasized to his brain.

567
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:26,995
There was nothing they could do.

568
00:36:29,108 --> 00:36:31,043
I was 21, and...

569
00:36:32,482 --> 00:36:34,417
I watched my husband die.

570
00:36:38,093 --> 00:36:40,063
I'm sorry.

571
00:36:44,239 --> 00:36:46,232
But that's not the whole story.

572
00:36:48,614 --> 00:36:51,278
It's a symptom, not your illness.

573
00:36:51,353 --> 00:36:55,019
Thyroid cancer would have been
diagnosed at least a year before his death.

574
00:36:55,094 --> 00:36:57,587
You knew he was dying
when you married him.

575
00:36:57,666 --> 00:37:00,466
It must have been
when you first met him.

576
00:37:03,845 --> 00:37:06,817
And you married him anyway.

577
00:37:08,522 --> 00:37:12,428
You can't be that good a person
and well-adjusted.

578
00:37:13,632 --> 00:37:16,330
Why?

579
00:37:16,404 --> 00:37:19,399
'Cause you wind up
crying over centrifuges.

580
00:37:22,283 --> 00:37:24,947
Or hating people?

581
00:37:25,022 --> 00:37:28,051
[Pager Beeping]

582
00:37:33,271 --> 00:37:34,967
[Pager Beeping]

583
00:37:39,952 --> 00:37:41,887
[Chase]
Her fever spiked at 104.

584
00:37:41,956 --> 00:37:43,891
Echo shows global hyperkinesis.

585
00:37:43,960 --> 00:37:45,953
Blood pressure?
Barely 90 over 40.

586
00:37:46,031 --> 00:37:49,937
Did you give her dopamine?
Started 270 micrograms
per minute 10 minutes ago.

587
00:37:50,006 --> 00:37:52,306
Still no change.

588
00:37:57,822 --> 00:38:01,922
Killing the parasites
isn't gonna do much good
if we kill her heart at the same time.

589
00:38:01,997 --> 00:38:04,399
A heart can be replaced.
A brain can't.

590
00:38:04,469 --> 00:38:06,769
Right now we're killing both.

591
00:38:06,840 --> 00:38:11,748
If she's gonna die, we should at least
let her do it without that acid
flowing through her veins.

592
00:38:11,816 --> 00:38:14,309
What's happening?

593
00:38:15,557 --> 00:38:20,123
We would've expected
your wife's condition to show
some improvement by now.

594
00:38:20,200 --> 00:38:22,135
It hasn't.

595
00:38:22,204 --> 00:38:24,197
It's going the other way.

596
00:38:55,371 --> 00:38:57,364
Please don't die.

597
00:38:59,279 --> 00:39:01,272
Please don't die.

598
00:39:06,928 --> 00:39:09,728
[Beeping]

599
00:39:09,801 --> 00:39:12,101
[Beeping Speed Increases]

600
00:39:26,434 --> 00:39:28,266
She's awake.

601
00:40:09,355 --> 00:40:12,258
Hey. She's gonna be okay.

602
00:40:12,328 --> 00:40:15,128
Yeah. Sure.

603
00:40:43,090 --> 00:40:45,925
What are you gonna do?

604
00:40:45,996 --> 00:40:48,523
Were you always honest with her?

605
00:40:48,601 --> 00:40:52,370
Do you know how lucky you are?
Your wife is alive. She loves you.

606
00:40:52,443 --> 00:40:55,415
What she did-

607
00:40:55,482 --> 00:40:58,385
You can't love a person
and do that to them.

608
00:41:01,094 --> 00:41:03,621
She loves you.

609
00:41:23,038 --> 00:41:25,736
[Sobbing]

610
00:41:40,239 --> 00:41:42,937
I need to know
who you had the affair with.

611
00:41:46,586 --> 00:41:49,421
He has to be notified
so he can get treatment as well.

612
00:41:53,767 --> 00:41:56,101
Why did you lie to us?

613
00:41:56,172 --> 00:41:58,665
You knew your life was at stake.

614
00:42:05,090 --> 00:42:07,492
He's not coming back, is he?

615
00:42:12,304 --> 00:42:14,536
We all make mistakes,

616
00:42:14,610 --> 00:42:16,580
and we all pay a price.

617
00:42:25,331 --> 00:42:27,665
I need that name.

618
00:42:33,347 --> 00:42:36,342
[Dog Barking In Distance]

619
00:42:41,463 --> 00:42:43,660
Hey, there.
How you doin'?

620
00:42:43,735 --> 00:42:45,670
Fine.

621
00:42:47,543 --> 00:42:49,513
Do you know where your dad is?

622
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:00,993
Can I help you?

623
00:43:50,771 --> 00:43:52,034
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
